    {
        "content": "<p>It's not clear to me that that is the correct definition outside of characteristic two. Would instead saying \"for any three points, at least two of the derivative maps coincide\" be better?</p>",
        "id": 604962668,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1781892961
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Seems unlikely this is a useful addition to Mathlib, too niche in my view.</p>",
        "id": 605277435,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1782010092
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you for your kind responses! <span aria-label=\"folded hands\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f64f\" role=\"img\" title=\"folded hands\">:folded_hands:</span></p>\n<p>Refactored it making it more general an reusable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/attilavjda/cautious-octo-barnacle/blob/main/DifferentialUniformityUpstreamSkeleton.lean\">DifferentialUniformityUpstreamSkeleton.lean</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>is something in this now perhaps a more general, reusable, more fit upstream for Mathlib?</p>\n<p>following your remark the previous definition is now:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ss\">`IsAtMostTwoToOne</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Among</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">any</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">three</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">points</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sharing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">derivative</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">value</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">least</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">two</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">coincide</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Needs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">only</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Sub</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">no</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">finiteness</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>the other is<br>\n<code>differentialUniformity</code> :<br>\nThe maximum fibre size of <code>f</code>'s derivative maps over all nonzero directions<br>\nOnly the domain <code>V</code> is required to be finite; the codomain <code>W</code> may be infinite.<br>\nand <code>IsAPN</code>, <code>IsPN</code> are now specialisations :</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Sub</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- **APN**: differential uniformity exactly `2`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsAPN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">differentialUniformity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- **PN** (planar / perfect nonlinear): differential uniformity exactly `1`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">differentialUniformity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and the nice invariant structural lemmas are:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c\">/-</span><span class=\"cm\">! ### Closure under post-composition with an additive equivalence -/</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">section</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Closure</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">derivMap_comp_addEquiv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">derivMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">derivMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fiberCard_comp_addEquiv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">fiberCard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fiberCard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">symm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Differential uniformity is invariant under post-composition with an additive</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">equivalence. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">differentialUniformity_comp_addEquiv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">differentialUniformity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">differentialUniformity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsAPN</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">comp_addEquiv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsAPN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsAPN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPN</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">comp_addEquiv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Closure</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'm just learning these concepts from formalising</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/attilavjda/cautious-octo-barnacle/tree/main/Kasami-APN-AB\">Kasami APN/AB</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>which already seems solid, and this additive bijection lemma seemed like one of the more reusable, invariant, structural patterns,</p>\n<p>as a special application, the additive bijection pattern is used in <a href=\"https://github.com/attilavjda/cautious-octo-barnacle/blob/main/Kasami-APN-AB/RequestProject/Core/KasamiEvenK.lean\">Kasami APN even formalisation</a>,<br>\nthe Frobenius twist move</p>\n<p>is any of this perhaps a better, more reusable, more fit upstream for Mathlib?</p>",
        "id": 605779439,
        "sender_full_name": "Attila Vajda",
        "timestamp": 1782144863
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for your interest in contributing to mathlib!<br>\nWe have discussed your question within mathlib's maintainers team: unfortunately, your proposed topic (APN maps, differential uniformities) is not suitable for mathlib --- as it is too specialized. As <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/contribute/index.html#what-to-contribute-to-mathlib\">explained in our documentation</a>, mathlib's scope is not \"all of mathematics\". Additionally, we don't have maintainers familiar with your material, so would not be able to maintain it over time.</p>\n<p>You can always develop this material in your own personal project.</p>",
        "id": 606999044,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1782661718
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"634338\">Michael Rothgang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/additive.20group.20APN.20lemma.20upstream.20question/near/606999044\">said</a> :</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Thanks for your interest in contributing to mathlib!<br>\nWe have discussed your question within mathlib's maintainers team: unfortunately, your proposed topic (APN maps, differential uniformities) is not suitable for mathlib --- as it is too specialized. As <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/contribute/index.html#what-to-contribute-to-mathlib\">explained in our documentation</a>, mathlib's scope is not \"all of mathematics\". Additionally, we don't have maintainers familiar with your material, so would not be able to maintain it over time.</p>\n<p>You can always develop this material in your own personal project.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Awesome, thanks a lot for discussing/considering it! I <em>am</em> developing it as an independent library too which seems meaningful</p>",
